{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfvZmYmiqa7wiEF9gVEVD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The sitting is recorded in two commits: the verdict `c5affece9` and the re-attestation of SR-148 `a89562749`. Both carry `WI: WI-902`, and the hooks passed. The verdict parses as a valid combined sitting (`SITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment;first-approval`).\n\n**The main finding: the adoption rules have two homes, and the code points at the one whose parents don't ask for them.**\n- **LLR-153** now describes the adoption sweep: the landing rule versus the replay rule, the re-point of dependents, and the refusals. Its `Module` and `CodeSymbol` cells claim `kitlib/adoption.py` and the related functions, and every one of those functions back-links `Implements: SR-174, LLR-153`. But its parents are SR-174 (identity allocated once) and SR-228 (held-rung re-attestation), and neither calls for adoption.\n- **LLR-335** traces to SR-148, which is the requirement that does call for adoption. But it restates the same rules, nine of its ten code symbols already belong to LLR-153, and the tenth is LLR-312's. No `Implements:` line anywhere names LLR-335.\n\n**Amendment: `VERDICT: MEANING rows=7`.** All seven rows add a case, a refusal or a check, so none is a clarity-only change.\n- **Blessed:** SR-148, LLR-161, TC-156, LLR-312 and TC-254.\n- **Not blessed:** LLR-153 and TC-147. Their parent chain is wrong for the new adoption rules, and the fix will touch them either way.\n\n**First approval: `OUTCOME: RETURN rows=2`.**\n- **LLR-335 returned:** it is the second home for the same decision. Its own point, one shared validator for both the close and the sweep, is worth keeping as the single home.\n- **TC-360 returned:** its row is returned. Its evidence also cites two title-matching tests that belong to LLR-153 and that TC-147 already cites.\n\n**Acts taken:**\n- **SR-148 re-anchored:** `intake.py snapshot --reattests SR-148`. It was the only drifted row in `system-requirements.toml`.\n- **Four blessed rows still unanchored:** LLR-161, LLR-312, TC-156 and TC-254. LLR-153's drift holds the LLR registry and TC-147's holds the TC registry, and the snapshot copies a registry whole. I didn't try to force them through.\n- **No approval act:** both first-approval rows are returned, so no Status was flipped.\n\n**Corrective work:** one `## Dispositions` draft, under `## amendment`, so that only one row gets minted. It asks the author to:\n- give the adoption rules one LLR home (LLR-335 recommended), with its symbols and back-links;\n- trace each rule to the parent that calls for it;\n- verify each rule in one TC;\n- re-sit LLR-153, TC-147, LLR-335 and TC-360 together with the four blessed-but-unanchored rows.\n\nThat draft only mints from an adjudication row's spec, so the coordinator needs to carry it to the lane. The untracked `006-ADJUDICATE-a52f1fb.md.requested` file is the coordinator's, and I left it as it was."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1786,"cache_read_input_tokens":159047,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1786},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73ae5cf9-d335-4bb7-a08f-7631da597f26","uuid":"676cf73d-f800-4d80-b899-ab93a02f95ee","timestamp":"2026-10-11T13:56:50.575Z","request_id":"req_011CfvZmYU7oNBWnwAhwZx6B"}
